# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Topen tag[2] fid[4] mode[1]
size[4] Ropen tag[2] qid[13] iounit[4]

size[4] Tcreate tag[2] fid[4] name[s] perm[4] mode[1]
size[4] Rcreate tag[2] qid[13] iounit[4]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`Topen` asks the server to check permissions and prepare <fid> for reading and writing with read(5)'s messages.
The low two bits of <mode> say how: 0 `OREAD` for reading, 1 `OWRITE` for writing, 2 `ORDWR` for both and 3 `OEXEC` for execution, each checked against the file's permissions.
`OTRUNC` (`0x10`) truncates the file as well, which needs write permission; `ORCLOSE` (`0x40`) asks that the file be removed when the fid is clunked, or when the connection ends; with the `posix` extension, when the last fid of its open file goes, so a fork's copies keep it until all are closed (M6 step 6d4c1). It needs a server that removes files: one without removal refuses the open with `permission denied`.
9Px adds `OAPPEND` (`0x80`) for the `posix` extension: the fid's open file appends, as below.
The bit `0x20` is the framework's own, never a client's, and is taken out of every mode a client sends; any other bit goes to the file server unchecked.
These messages are 9P2000's, types 112 to 115, unchanged on the wire, and need no extension.

<fid> must be known and not already open.
A directory may be opened only to read or execute: one opened for writing or with `OTRUNC` is refused before the file server sees it.
The file server checks the rest, at the open: fsd(4) asks `r` for reading, `w` for writing or truncating and `x` for execution, as Plan 9's servers do, and a later change to the file's permissions does not change what an open fid may do.

An open may move the fid.
Opening a clone file, such as netd(4)'s `/net/tcp/clone`, makes a new file, the new connection's `ctl`, and the fid then refers to that, open; <qid> in `Ropen` is the new file's.
An open the server cannot make yet, such as a `listen` file's before a call comes, gets no reply until it can, and the fid is not open until then.

With the `posix` extension (see version(5)), opening a file that is not a directory makes an open file at the server: the file, the mode, an offset that `Tread` and `Twrite` at `P9_OFFSET_CURRENT` use, and whether writes append, which `OAPPEND` sets from the start.
share(5) describes how other fids, on any of the server's connections, come to share it.
A server keeps 256 open files across all its connections, and an open when they are all in use fails.

`Tcreate` asks the server to make a file called <name> in the directory <fid> refers to, and then open it with <mode>, as `Topen` would; <fid> then refers to the new file, open.
It needs write permission in the directory.
<fid> must be a directory, known and not open.
<name> follows walk(5)'s rules, and may not be `..` either.
The `DMDIR` bit (`0x80000000`) of <perm> makes a directory, which may be opened only `OREAD`.
A name already in the directory is refused: `Tcreate` never truncates an existing file, so a client wanting that walks to it and opens it with `OTRUNC`.
The `posix` extension's open file is made as `Topen` makes it, `OAPPEND` included.

The file server decides the new file's owner and permissions.
fsd(4) does as Plan 9's servers do: the owner is the request's user, the group is the directory's, and the permissions are <perm> `& (~0666 | (dir.perm & 0666))` for a file and <perm> `& (dir.perm & 0777)` for a directory, so a file may have no permission its directory denies.
tmpfs(4) keeps <perm> `& 0777`, and has no owners.
Every VectraOS server keeps only `DMDIR` and the low nine bits of <perm>.

The <iounit> in `Ropen` and `Rcreate` is the negotiated msize less 24, `P9_IOHDRSZ`: the most one `Tread` or `Twrite` carries.
VectraOS's servers always send it, never zero.

